WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.981
- [Narrator] This is Peter,
and he's a giant anteater.

00:00:03.981 --> 00:00:06.648
(playful music)

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.240
The giant anteater is
the largest of the four

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.380
anteater species, reaching
up to seven feet in length.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.470
Anteaters are edentates,
which is a fancy way

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:20.220
of saying they have no teeth.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.900
But with a tongue that can
reach two feet in length,

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.051
eating is never a problem.

00:00:25.051 --> 00:00:27.750
In the wild, they enjoy a diet of ants,

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.800
termites, grubs, and the occasional fruit.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.440
Here in captivity, Peter chows down

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.280
on this insect porridge and yogurt.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.260
Their tongue can flick up to
150 times a minute, letting

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.420
them gobble up tens of
thousands of insects each day.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.460
The giant anteater's four-inch claws

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.690
and muscular forelegs are
used to tear apart anthills

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.010
and termite mounds, but
also to fend off predators

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.650
like mountain lions and jaguars.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.480
The species is currently vulnerable

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.040
due to over hunting by
humans and habitat loss.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.620
The giant anteater dates
back 25 million years,

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.900
so conversation efforts are working hard

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.690
to keep Peter and his species around.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.891
This is the giant anteater.

00:01:11.891 --> 00:01:14.558
(playful music)

